  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nRising pressure heights over surface high pressure will allow\narea temperature to return closer to normal today and ever so\nslightly above normal Friday while providing dry weather and\nabundant sunshine.\n\nThis pattern changes as an upper trough drops southeast out of\nthe northern Rockies towards the Ohio River Valley by Friday\nevening before crossing the region by early next week. The\ncombination of jet dynamics providing lift with weak moisture\nadvection ahead of its axis results in increased shower and\nthunderstorm chances. Little more can be said at this point due\nto large variances in the shape and eastward progression of this\ntrough creating different precipitation timing (along with\nenvironmental threats).  \n\nThe range of outcomes seems to be bounded by 1) a more \nprogressive trough hinted by the initial 00z NAM model run that\nresults in storm coverage peaking by Saturday \nafternoon/evening; and 2) a slower and slightly deeper trough \nfavored by a healthy portion of GEFS ensemble members that \nresults in storms peaking Sunday morning/afternoon. Ensemble\nclusters generally fall in between these two ranges (peaking\nlate Saturday into early Sunday) and where the current forecast\naligns. Due to this variance, precipitation chances could vary\nin the coming days as models attempt to come in alignment.\nAdditional uncertainty arises in the timing of trough exiting,\nranging from Sunday afternoon/evening to Tuesday. So long-range\nPoP outlooks may also experience similar fluctuations in the\ncoming days.\n\nRegardless of the scenarios, the overall hazard risk appears low\nas buoyancy is likely to be limited (outside of a narrow\ncorridor near the Laurel Highlands on Sunday, probability of\nSBCAPE being less than 1000 J/kg is greater than 80%). The only\nhazard to monitor is flood risks as there could be enough of a\nGulf moisture draw to push PWAT values near/above the 90th\npercentile; if this occurs while storm motion is slow and enough\ninstability manifests to aid rainfall rates, localized flash\nflooding risks could materialize.",
